@h1 Rendering: HTML, Markdown & PDF h1@

One source, several outputs. The same @code .wit code@ file becomes a web page, a
Markdown file for a repository, or a paginated PDF for a reader — each with a
single command. This page covers what each format is good for and every option
for producing it.

@h2 The default: a finished HTML document h2@

The plain build command prints a complete, self-contained, styled HTML document
to your terminal:

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit
pre@@@

"Self-contained" means the theme's CSS is inlined into the page head — one file
you can open in any browser or email, with no external stylesheet to ship
alongside. Redirect it to a file, or use the output flag below.

@h2 Embedding: a bare fragment h2@

When you want to drop the content into a page you already have — a blog template,
a CMS — ask for a fragment instead:

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit --fragment
pre@@@

That emits just the article element, with no page head and no theme, ready to
nest inside your own HTML.

@h2 Choosing the output format h2@

Two ways to pick a format. The output flag infers the format from the file
extension:

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit -o essay.html     ~ HTML
wit build essay.wit -o essay.md       ~ Markdown
wit build essay.wit -o essay.pdf      ~ PDF
pre@@@

An unrecognised extension is an error rather than a guess. To force a format
regardless of extension, name it explicitly:

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit --format md
pre@@@

@h2 Markdown h2@

Markdown output gives you GitHub-flavoured Markdown — headings, bold and italic,
lists, a pipe table, blockquotes. It is the right choice for a README, an issue,
or anywhere portable plain text is wanted. Remember that a few HTML-only features
degrade: a hard line break becomes a space, and embedded audio or video has no
Markdown equivalent.

@h2 PDF h2@

PDF renders the same document through a headless browser into a paginated,
print-ready file. It is the format to send a reader or hand to a printer. It
needs a local Chrome or Chromium (or one you point to with an environment
variable); without a browser, the build stops and tells you to render HTML and
convert instead.

@h2 The raw base h2@

By default your document carries the batteries-included theme. Swap it for a
mechanical reset — a blank canvas you style yourself — with the raw flag:

@@@pre
wit build essay.wit --raw
pre@@@

Raw output keeps the document shell but drops the theme, leaving only a minimal
reset. Reach for it when you want total art direction — see
@a |href /docs/write/styling/| Styling a@.

@h2 Building with live data h2@

A document can pull in data produced at build time — a table of figures, a team
roster — through a load node wired to a data source. Building such a document
needs a sources file and permission to run the program that produces the data:

@@@pre
wit build report.wit --sources wit.sources.json --allow-exec --env .env
pre@@@

The loaded data flows into lists, tables, and headings exactly as if you had
typed it. The full external-data machinery lives in the Guides section; this is
the writer's-eye view of what the flags do.

@h2 Which format when h2@

@table |rows [[Format, Reach for it when, Watch out for], [HTML, sharing or embedding on the web, nothing — it is the native target], [Markdown, a README or a repository, media and hard breaks degrade to text], [PDF, a reader or the printer, needs a local browser to render]]|

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Expecting the default output to be a fragment._ The plain build is a
finished document; use the fragment flag to embed. li@
@li _Expecting media in Markdown or PDF._ Audio and video are HTML-only. li@
@li _An unknown output extension._ The output flag errors on an extension it does
not recognise; use a known one or force the format. li@
ul@

